# Generated API contract. The SDK drift gate compares the committed file byte
# for byte against a freshly generated one, and the generator emits LF. Without
# this rule a Windows checkout (core.autocrlf=true) rewrites it to CRLF, so the
# gate reports permanent drift that regenerating cannot fix — the commit would
# just be converted back. Pin it to LF on every platform.
sdk/openapi.json text eol=lf

# Shell scripts MUST keep LF endings. They are COPYed into Linux images and run
# as container entrypoints; a Windows checkout (core.autocrlf=true) rewrites them
# to CRLF, so the shebang becomes "#!/bin/sh\r" and the container dies with
# "exec /app/docker/backend-entrypoint.sh: no such file or directory". CI/prod
# build on Linux (LF) and were unaffected; only local Windows Docker builds broke.
*.sh text eol=lf

# Generated SDK clients. They are produced with LF by the Linux Docker
# generators and compared for reproducibility; a Windows checkout
# (core.autocrlf=true) would rewrite them to CRLF and manufacture phantom diffs.
# Pin the whole generated tree to LF on every platform.
sdk/typescript/** text eol=lf
sdk/python/** text eol=lf

# Generated i18n projection. The drift gate compares the committed files byte
# for byte against freshly generated ones, and the generator emits LF. Same trap
# as sdk/openapi.json above: a Windows checkout (core.autocrlf=true) rewrites
# them to CRLF, so the gate reports drift that regenerating cannot fix, because
# the regenerated LF file is converted straight back on the next checkout.
backend/i18n/*.json text eol=lf

# The generator itself is read on Linux CI and Windows alike; keep it LF so the
# file it writes and the file it compares are produced the same way everywhere.
scripts/generate-i18n-projection.mjs text eol=lf
